## Decisions

**1. `would-overwrite` as a first-class planning status, not a boolean flag.**
The spec is unambiguous: `would-overwrite` is the fourth enum value, sibling
to `would-create`. A boolean like `hasOverwrite` on the step would force the
agent to branch on two independent fields (`status === "would-create" &&
hasOverwrite`) instead of one. String-literal-union enums are the playbook's
default for domain values (see `workflow/type-system.md`), and
`InitStepStatus` is already a string literal union — extending it is the
natural fit.

**2. `"overwritten"` sits next to `"created"` in the execution-status
family.** The spec names three manifest statuses: `"created"`, `"overwritten"`,
`"exists"`. These are parallel to upgrade's `"updated"`, `"created"`,
`"unchanged"` (see `UpgradeFileStatus`), and `"overwritten"` is the
semantically correct verb for a destructive write the agent approved. The
alternative — reusing `"created"` for both fresh writes and overwrites —
would collapse the truthful reporting the spec explicitly requires
("created 6, overwrote 1" vs. the bad example of "installed 7 files").

**3. Byte-compare lives in a new `shared/compareBytes/` helper, not inlined
per category.** Seven category helpers need the same three-way decision
(missing / identical / drifted). Inlining the read + compare in each one
would duplicate the ENOENT handling, the encoding choice, and the
return-type shape seven ways — exactly the "god helper" the playbook's
"aggressive modularization" rule exists to prevent. A shared submodule
inside `src/tools/init/shared/` is the playbook's nearest-common-ancestor
escalation path; it is not lifted to `src/lib/` because only init consumes
it. (Upgrade has its own `compareFile` with a different return-type
contract and lives alongside upgrade's helpers. The two do not converge —
init's three-value return matches init's three planning statuses; upgrade's
status surface is different.)

**4. `would-skip` stays distinct from `exists` in the planning layer but
collapses to `exists` for category-owned content.** The spec lists four
planning statuses: `would-create`, `would-overwrite`, `would-skip`,
`exists`. `would-skip` is still useful for steps where the tool
deliberately declines to act (canonical read failure, VS Code CLI absent,
Zed JSON parse failure) — they are not the same as a file that is already
at canonical bytes. For the seven category helpers that write canonical
content, `compareBytes → "would-skip"` (bytes already match canonical)
becomes `status: "exists"` in the returned `InitStep`, because for these
helpers "same bytes as canonical" is definitionally "already set up."
`would-skip` is reserved for the "I can't or won't act" branch. This is
named in each helper's step in §3–§7 and recorded here so the implementor
does not map all three `compareBytes` outcomes 1:1 to the status enum.

**5. `would-skip` manifest survival.** The spec's desired-outcome list
names `"created"`, `"overwritten"`, `"exists"` as the manifest statuses.
That list is the set of *new* statuses the manifest carries after apply
— it does not say `"would-skip"` disappears. A step that was already
`"would-skip"` before `applySteps` stays `"would-skip"` after, because
`applySteps` writes nothing for it. Dropping `would-skip` would silently
mask the "canonical read failed, skipping this one" signal the agent
should surface. Recorded here because it is subtle and would be easy to
remove "for cleanliness."

**6. `writeMethodology` (pointer stub) uses a marker-aware drift check,
not a straight `compareBytes`.** The pointer stub is embedded inside the
host's agent config file (e.g. `CLAUDE.md`), surrounded by user-authored
content. A whole-file byte-compare against `composeStub(docHubDir)` would
always fail because the user has other content in that file. The drift
check extracts the bytes between the marker pair and compares those to
the canonical stub. This logic is too narrow to live in
`shared/compareBytes` — it belongs next to the stub logic that owns the
marker convention.

**7. README badge detection stays a substring check.** The spec and the
existing scaffoldReadme helper use `BADGE_DETECTION` substring presence
as the "already installed" signal — full-file byte-compare is
inappropriate because the README has user-authored content around the
badge. README therefore cannot produce `would-overwrite`; the possible
statuses remain `would-create` (no README, create it with badge; or
README present but badge absent, inject it) and `exists` (badge already
present). The spec does not require a `would-overwrite` path for README,
and the scaffoldReadme `.aide` already states "the two statuses are
`would-create` and `exists`."

**8. `InitCategory` is not extended — upgrade lockstep is preserved.**
Spec `outcomes.undesired[14]` warns about drift between init's and
upgrade's category lists. This plan adds no new category. The existing
mismatch (init has `framework` and `brain`; upgrade does not) is
deliberate — framework detection and brain provisioning are init-only
concerns that have no upgrade counterpart. Step 10b verifies the
`category` enum matches `InitCategory` so no accidental category slips
in while rewriting the tool description.

**9. The `/aide:init` slash-command prompt update is out-of-scope for
this plan.** The prompt file lives at `.claude/commands/aide/init.md`,
outside `src/tools/init/`. Per the single-module-scoping rule
(`plan-aide.md`), the plan only covers work inside its scope directory.
That file gets its own plan; this plan declares it as a prerequisite and
describes the contract (silent-apply-when-pure-create, AskUserQuestion
when overwrite-bearing, always-prompt for inherent decisions) so the
companion plan has a clear target.

**10. No ordering or category-graph changes.** The orchestrator's
top-level flow (run every category helper, assemble `steps[]`, return
`InitResult`) is unchanged. Each helper still plans its own steps in
isolation. The new status value flows through without touching any
inter-category coupling — "category isolation" from the spec's Strategy
section is already the structure and nothing about this change disturbs
it.

**11. VS Code IDE steps are exempt from the byte-compare branch.** The
`configureVscode` helper reports `would-create` / `exists` / `would-skip`
based on CLI availability and extension install state — it does not own a
file the tool writes, so "bytes on disk drift from canonical" is not a
meaningful question. `applySteps` continues to pass VS Code steps through
with an `instructions` field. No change needed in step 6 for the VS Code
path.
